 "result_text": "`Git.refresh` (and so `git.refresh`) now invalidates the cached `version_info` on every `Git` instance. I didn't add or run a test of the stale case.\n\nI only ran a quick check: it reads `version_info`, calls `git.refresh()`, then reads it again. That check can't show the fix working, because both reads return `(2, 47, 3)`. The issue's two-git scenario needs a second git binary on the machine, and I didn't try it.\n\n`Git.refresh` in `git/cmd.py` now sets a new class-level marker each time it runs. Each instance records the marker value when it computes `version_info`. The property drops the cached value and recomputes it if the marker has changed. I updated the docstring to say the cache is per-instance but cleared by a refresh. The new per-instance attribute is excluded from pickling, like `_version_info`.\n\nThe marker also changes when `refresh` is called with a bad path and then reverts to the old executable. Instances then re-read their version once more, which gives the same answer.",
